.TH CALL_TCP 1 "12 October 2026" Linux "FPAC Operator's Manual"
.SH NAME
call_tcp \- links a user of the node to the telnet port of a BBS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B call_tcp host [port]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.B call_tcp
opens a TCP connection to
.I host
and gives the callsign of the calling user at the login prompt of the BBS,
then relays the data both ways until one side closes.
.P
It is not a daemon: it is run for each call, by
.B fpad
for a Command or an Application of
.I fpac.conf,
or by
.B ax25d.
The callsign is the one of the AX.25, NET/ROM or ROSE station connected to
its standard output; for a user who came in by telnet it is read from the
environment variable CALL_TCP set by the node. Run from a terminal, it asks
for the callsign.
.P
The callsign is sent when the BBS output contains "callsign" or "login",
preceded by a dot except when run from a terminal. What the BBS sends
before that prompt is not shown; what follows (a password prompt, if any) is
relayed to the user.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 10
.BI host
name or IP address of the BBS.
.TP 10
.BI port
TCP port number or service name. Default: telnet (23).
.TP 10
.BI "\-h, \-?"
show a short help and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
In fpac.conf:
.EX
Command
	BBs = /usr/local/sbin/call_tcp mybbs.ampr.org 6300
End
.EE
.SH NOTES
The BBS believes the callsign it is given: its telnet port should accept
connections from the node only.
.SH AUTHOR
Bernard Pidoux F6BVP <bernard.f6bvp@gmail.com>
.SH SEE ALSO
